  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nPersistent upper high remains fixed over New Mexico and West \nTexas, leading to drier and much warmer-than-normal weather \nconditions for the end of July. GFS 500-mb height anomalies \nsuggest this high is 3-4 standard deviations stronger than is \ntypical for the middle of the North American Monsoon. The pattern \nis not favorable for widespread rain, as any storms that do form \nwill be mostly driven by recycled moisture and convective \ntemperature/terrain forcing producing meager lift. The primary \nmonsoonal moisture plume is focused further west, mostly over \nSonora and southern Arizona.\n\nThe main headline for the desert lowlands will continue to be hot\ntemperatures. Suffocating upper ridge aloft will promote very hot\nafternoon conditions, followed by little relief overnight. Highs \nwill run 8-12 degrees above normal for late July, which means \nlowland highs in the 102 to 108 degree range through at least \nFriday. A Heat Advisory is in effect for El Paso, the lower Rio \nGrande Valley, and portions of south-central New Mexico on\nThursday, and may need to be expanded further across southern New\nMexico on Friday.\n\nDowntrend in storm chances continues on Thursday, with \nprecipitable water values in the 0.9-1.1\" range (slightly below \nnormal). The upper high directly over central New Mexico will act \nas a suppressant for rain chances. Subsident 500-mb flow will \ncause a warming effect aloft and eat away at potential \ninstability. Southwest New Mexico and the high terrain may still \nsee isolated (10-30% coverage) showers and thunderstorms Thursday \nafternoon, but the majority of the CWA will remain dry.\n\nRain chances do increase Friday/Saturday. Upper high will make a \nretrograde maneuver, shifting west over the lower Colorado River \nvalley. At the same time, a surface boundary moving down the \nPlains will push additional moisture southwest into the El Paso \nregion. PW's in the 1.3-1.5\" range should return by Friday, \nallowing for more scattered (50-70% coverage) showers and \nthunderstorms. Current models suggest Friday could be a high \nflooding risk with very slow storm motions, followed by a higher \nsevere threat Saturday given north flow aloft and marginal 0-6km \nshear.\n\nThe start of next week looks drier again, though temperatures will\nbe sub-Advisory (lowlands highs 98-102). Next week's rain chances\nonce again look to favor the high elevations and western New\nMexico, with much of the Rio Grande Valley and Tularosa Basin \nstaying dry most evenings.",
